{"tool_name":"saigegds","contents":["oeb","biotools","bioschemas"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"saigegds","home":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/SNPRelate","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Genome-wide association studies (GWAS) are widely used to investigate the genetic basis of diseases and traits, but they pose many computational challenges. We developed an R package SNPRelate to provide a binary format for single-nucleotide polymorphism (SNP) data in GWAS utilizing CoreArray Genomic Data Structure (GDS) data files. The GDS format offers the efficient operations specifically designed for integers with two bits, since a SNP could occupy only two bits. SNPRelate is also designed to accelerate two key computations on SNP data using parallel computing for multi-core symmetric multiprocessing computer architectures: Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and relatedness analysis using Identity-By-Descent measures. The SNP GDS format is also used by the GWASTools package with the support of S4 classes and generic functions. The extended GDS format is implemented in the SeqArray package to support the storage of single nucleotide variations (SNVs), insertion/deletion polymorphism (indel) and structural variation calls in whole-genome and whole-exome variant data.","addition_date":"2021-01-18T10:48:31Z","last_update_date":"2026-09-13T07:36:05.629867Z","version":["1.46.0"],"collections":["BioConductor"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:saigegds","credit":[{"name":"Bruce Weir","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Cathy Laurie","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Stephanie Gogarten","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Contributor"]},{"name":"Xiuwen Zheng","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Developer","Maintainer"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/SNPRelate"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Genotyping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3196"},{"term":"Imputation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3557"},{"term":"Regression analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3659"}]}],"name":"SAIGEgds","owner":"zsmag19","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/bts606"}],"topic":[{"term":"Biobank","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3337"},{"term":"DNA polymorphism","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2885"},{"term":"GWAS study","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3517"},{"term":"Genotype and phenotype","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0625"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"SAIGEgds","home":"https://bio.tools/saigegds","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Scalable Implementation of Generalized mixed models using GDS files in Phenome-Wide Association Studies.\n\nScalable implementation of generalized mixed models with highly optimized C++ implementation and integration with Genomic Data Structure (GDS) files. It is designed for single variant tests in large-scale phenome-wide association studies (PheWAS) with millions of variants and samples, controlling for sample structure and case-control imbalance. The implementation is based on the original SAIGE R package (v0.29.4.4). SAIGEgds also implements some of the SPAtest functions in C to speed up the calculation of Saddlepoint approximation. Benchmarks show that SAIGEgds is 5 to 6 times faster than the original SAIGE R package.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:saigegds/undefined/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":false}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":22,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":2021},{"count":2,"year":2022},{"count":2,"year":2026},{"count":4,"year":2023},{"count":6,"year":2024},{"count":7,"year":2025}],"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btaa731","pmid":"32898220","ref_count":0,"refs":[],"title":"SAIGEgds-an efficient statistical tool for large-scale PheWAS with mixed models.","year":2021}]}],"summary":{"description":true},"website":{"bioschemas":false,"copyright":false,"https":true,"license":false,"operational":200,"redirects":[{"status":301,"url":"https://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/SAIGEgds.html"},{"status":302,"url":"http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/SAIGEgds.html"}],"ssl":true,"ssl_err":null}}},{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:saigegds:1.48.2/undefined/bioconductor.org","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","project":{"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":85,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":2017},{"count":4,"year":2018},{"count":6,"year":2020},{"count":6,"year":2023},{"count":8,"year":2026},{"count":9,"year":2019},{"count":11,"year":2022},{"count":12,"year":2021},{"count":12,"year":2024},{"count":16,"year":2025}],"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btx145","pmcid":"PMC5860110","pmid":"28334390","ref_count":26,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":1984},{"count":1,"year":2002},{"count":1,"year":2004},{"count":1,"year":2005},{"count":1,"year":2006},{"count":1,"year":2007},{"count":1,"year":2009},{"count":1,"year":2012},{"count":1,"year":2013},{"count":2,"year":2011},{"count":2,"year":2014},{"count":3,"year":2010},{"count":3,"year":2016},{"count":7,"year":2015}],"title":"SeqArray-a storage-efficient high-performance data format for WGS variant calls.","year":2017}]},{"entries":[{"cit_count":1371,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":2012},{"count":2,"year":-1},{"count":3,"year":2013},{"count":17,"year":2014},{"count":23,"year":2015},{"count":46,"year":2016},{"count":62,"year":2017},{"count":92,"year":2018},{"count":113,"year":2019},{"count":113,"year":2020},{"count":113,"year":2026},{"count":122,"year":2023},{"count":146,"year":2024},{"count":163,"year":2021},{"count":163,"year":2025},{"count":192,"year":202